## Why This Is Asked
Assesses design for fair throughput, scalability, and auditable enforcement in a shared data lake.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant quotas; token bucket; API Gateway usage plans; DynamoDB; Lambda; S3 prefixes; CloudWatch; Kinesis Firehose; testing at scale.

## Code Example
```javascript
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(rate, burst) {
    this.rate = rate; // tokens per second
    this.burst = burst;
    this.tokens = burst;
    this.last = Date.now();
  }
  allow(n=1){
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = (now - this.last) / 1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.burst, this.tokens + elapsed * this.rate);
    this.last = now;
    if (this.tokens >= n) { this.tokens -= n; return true; }
    return false;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test multi-tenant fairness under bursty conditions and validate SLA guarantees?
- What changes if large tenants typically spike during business hours?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical serverless design, idempotency, error handling, and IAM least privilege in a real-world image workflow.

## Key Concepts

- S3 event triggers and Lambda integration
- Idempotent design using a dedupe key stored in DynamoDB
- Least-privilege IAM roles for S3, Lambda, and DynamoDB
- Dead Letter Queue (DLQ) setup for failed events

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal idempotent Lambda outline
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const ddb = new AWS.DynamoDB.DocumentClient();
// image processing omitted
exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const rec = event.Records[0];
  const key = recs.s3.object.key;
  const jobKey = key;
  const exists = await ddb.get({TableName:'processed', Key:{jobKey}}).promise();
  if (exists.Item) return {status:'skipped'};
  // resize and store output placeholder
  await ddb.put({TableName:'processed', Item:{jobKey, t: Date.now()}}).promise();
  return {status:'done'};
};
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test idempotency under concurrent uploads?
- How would you monitor DLQ failures and ensure timely retries?

